Real code:

public abstract class FoodCreator {

    protected abstract FoodItem createFood(Point position);

    public final FoodItem spawnAt(Point position) {
        return createFood(position);
    }
}

public class NormalFoodCreator extends FoodCreator {
    @Override
    protected FoodItem createFood(Point position) { return new NormalFood(position); }
}

public class BonusFoodCreator extends FoodCreator {
    @Override
    protected FoodItem createFood(Point position) { return new BonusFood(position); }
}

There is no if/else and no switch anywhere in this creation code. Given a FoodCreator reference, what decides which kind of FoodItem you get?,A type parameter passed to spawnAt(),"Which subclass the reference actually points at - NormalFoodCreator builds NormalFood, BonusFoodCreator builds BonusFood, and the choice was made when the creator object was chosen","The position, since different cells produce different foods",A static registry inside FoodCreator,,2,"The branching is replaced by object choice. Whoever picked the creator has already made the decision, so no code needs to re-examine it later.",2

