public class BonusFoodCreator extends FoodCreator {
    @Override
    protected FoodItem createFood(Point position) { return new BonusFood(position); }
}

spawnAt() is declared final and simply calls createFood(). Why make a one-line method final rather than letting subclasses override it?,To make it run faster,"It fixes the public entry point so subclasses can only change HOW the item is built, not what happens around the building - the same parent-controls-the-shape idea used by performMove() and handleBoundary()",Because createFood() is protected,Because Java requires public methods in abstract classes to be final,,2,"It is a miniature of the template method used elsewhere in this project: a fixed public step calling a replaceable internal one. Right now it wraps nothing extra, but that is where shared pre- or post-work would go.",2

Real code:

public abstract class FoodItem {
    private final Point position;
    private final int points;

    protected FoodItem(Point position, int points) {
        this.position = position;
        this.points = points;
    }
}

public class NormalFood extends FoodItem {
    private static final int POINTS = 1;
    public NormalFood(Point position) { super(position, POINTS); }
}

public class BonusFood extends FoodItem {
    private static final int POINTS = 3;
    public BonusFood(Point position) { super(position, POINTS); }
}

public abstract class FoodCreator {

    protected abstract FoodItem createFood(Point position);

    public final FoodItem spawnAt(Point position) {
        return createFood(position);
    }
}

public class NormalFoodCreator extends FoodCreator {
    @Override
    protected FoodItem createFood(Point position) { return new NormalFood(position); }
}

public class BonusFoodCreator extends FoodCreator {
    @Override
    protected FoodItem createFood(Point position) { return new BonusFood(position); }
}

Adding SuperFood (worth 5) required SuperFood and SuperFoodCreator. Which existing files had to be edited?,"FoodItem and FoodCreator, to register the new type","None. Both new classes plug into the existing abstract classes, and NormalFood, BonusFood, their creators and SnakeGame were all untouched","SnakeGame, to add the new points value","IFoodSpawnStrategy, to add a new method",,2,"Two parallel hierarchies, one new leaf in each. This is the measurable version of 'a new food kind must be addable in isolation' from the requirements.",1

Real code:

public interface IFoodSpawnStrategy {
    FoodCreator nextFoodCreator();
}

public class RandomFoodSpawnStrategy implements IFoodSpawnStrategy {
    private static final double BONUS_PROBABILITY = 0.2;

    @Override
    public FoodCreator nextFoodCreator() {
        return random.nextDouble() < BONUS_PROBABILITY ? bonusCreator : normalCreator;
    }
}

public class BonusEveryNFoodSpawnStrategy implements IFoodSpawnStrategy {
    private final int n;
    private int spawnCount = 0;

    @Override
    public FoodCreator nextFoodCreator() {
        spawnCount++;
        return spawnCount % n == 0 ? bonusCreator : normalCreator;
    }
}

private FoodItem spawnFood() {
    Point position;
    do {
        position = new Point(random.nextInt(board.getHeight()), random.nextInt(board.getWidth()));
    } while (occupied.contains(position));
    return foodSpawnStrategy.nextFoodCreator().spawnAt(position);
}

The strategy answers 'which kind of food comes next' and the creator answers 'how that kind is built'. Why keep these as two separate abstractions instead of one?,They should be one - splitting them is over-engineering,They change for different reasons. Adding a food type adds a creator without touching any strategy

Real code:

public interface IFoodSpawnStrategy {
    FoodCreator nextFoodCreator();
}

public class RandomFoodSpawnStrategy implements IFoodSpawnStrategy {
    private static final double BONUS_PROBABILITY = 0.2;

    @Override
    public FoodCreator nextFoodCreator() {
        return random.nextDouble() < BONUS_PROBABILITY ? bonusCreator : normalCreator;
    }
}

public class BonusEveryNFoodSpawnStrategy implements IFoodSpawnStrategy {
    private final int n;
    private int spawnCount = 0;

    @Override
    public FoodCreator nextFoodCreator() {
        spawnCount++;
        return spawnCount % n == 0 ? bonusCreator : normalCreator;
    }
}

Compare the two strategies. One consults a random number; the other keeps a spawnCount field and increments it. What difference does that make when the same strategy object is reused for a second game?,No difference - both behave identically on a new game,"The counting strategy carries its count into the second game, so the second game starts mid-cycle rather than at the beginning. The random one has no such carry-over from the count, though a shared Random continues its sequence too",The counting strategy resets automatically when a new game is constructed,"Neither can be reused, because both are stateless",,2,"Verified by calling one shared strategy four times: Normal, Bonus, Super, Normal. Whatever a previous game consumed, the next game inherits, which is exactly the state leak the requirements forbid between games.",2

