Following on: nothing in the code stops someone reusing one strategy instance across two games. What would prevent this mistake?,Making nextFoodCreator() static,"Either give the strategy a reset() the game calls at construction, or have SnakeGame take a factory that produces a fresh strategy per game - anything that makes 'fresh per game' the default rather than a rule to remember",Making spawnCount volatile,Nothing can prevent it

Real code:

private FoodItem spawnFood() {
    Point position;
    do {
        position = new Point(random.nextInt(board.getHeight()), random.nextInt(board.getWidth()));
    } while (occupied.contains(position));
    return foodSpawnStrategy.nextFoodCreator().spawnAt(position);
}

Trace this method. Which part chooses WHERE the food goes, and which part chooses WHAT it is?,The strategy chooses both position and type,"The do/while loop picks the position - a random free cell, chosen by SnakeGame itself - and only the type is delegated, to the strategy and then the creator",The creator chooses the position via spawnAt(),The board chooses the position and the strategy chooses the type,,2,"Worth noticing that position selection was never made swappable. A game wanting food to appear only near the edges would have to modify SnakeGame, because that rule has no seam.",2

Real code:

private FoodItem spawnFood() {
    Point position;
    do {
        position = new Point(random.nextInt(board.getHeight()), random.nextInt(board.getWidth()));
    } while (occupied.contains(position));
    return foodSpawnStrategy.nextFoodCreator().spawnAt(position);
}

Following on: suppose you now want food placement to be configurable - random, or always in the half of the board furthest from the snake. What does the current code force you to do?,Add a new FoodCreator subclass,"Edit SnakeGame itself, because the placement rule is hardcoded in this private method with no interface in front of it - unlike the type rule, which already has IFoodSpawnStrategy",Add a new value to the Direction enum,Nothing - IFoodSpawnStrategy already controls placement,,2,"A good illustration that extensibility is never general: this design is open to new food types and new boundary rules, and closed to new placement rules. Which seams exist is a choice that was made, deliberately or not.",2

Real code:

private FoodItem spawnFood() {
    Point position;
    do {
        position = new Point(random.nextInt(board.getHeight()), random.nextInt(board.getWidth()));
    } while (occupied.contains(position));
    return foodSpawnStrategy.nextFoodCreator().spawnAt(position);
}

The loop rejects any position already occupied by the snake. What game rule does that enforce, and which other piece of code silently depends on it?,It enforces that food is always reachable

Real code:

public abstract class FoodItem {
    private final Point position;
    private final int points;

    protected FoodItem(Point position, int points) {
        this.position = position;
        this.points = points;
    }
}

public class NormalFood extends FoodItem {
    private static final int POINTS = 1;
    public NormalFood(Point position) { super(position, POINTS); }
}

public class BonusFood extends FoodItem {
    private static final int POINTS = 3;
    public BonusFood(Point position) { super(position, POINTS); }
}

Points are fixed per subclass: 1 for NormalFood, 3 for BonusFood, 5 for SuperFood. A designer now wants bonus food worth 3 on easy boards and 6 on hard ones. What does this structure make awkward?,Nothing - a static final can be changed at runtime,"The value is baked into the class rather than supplied to the object, so a second value needs either another subclass or a constructor parameter the creators currently have no way to supply",FoodItem would have to become an interface,The creators would need to be singletons,,2,The creator layer is where this would be fixed - a BonusFoodCreator taking the point value as a constructor argument handles any number of variants with no new food classes.,2

Real code:

public interface IFoodSpawnStrategy {
    FoodCreator nextFoodCreator();
}

public class RandomFoodSpawnStrategy implements IFoodSpawnStrategy {
    private static final double BONUS_PROBABILITY = 0.2;

    @Override
    public FoodCreator nextFoodCreator() {
        return random.nextDouble() < BONUS_PROBABILITY ? bonusCreator : normalCreator;
    }
}

public class BonusEveryNFoodSpawnStrategy implements IFoodSpawnStrategy {
    private final int n;
    private int spawnCount = 0;

    @Override
    public FoodCreator nextFoodCreator() {
        spawnCount++;
        return spawnCount % n == 0 ? bonusCreator : normalCreator;
    }
}

Both strategies build their own creator objects internally, e.g. 'private final FoodCreator bonusCreator = new BonusFoodCreator();'. What does that prevent a caller from doing?,Nothing - the caller never needs to see the creators,"Supplying different creators - for example a strategy that alternates Normal and Super rather than Normal and Bonus would need a new strategy class, even though its selection logic is identical",Reusing the same strategy for two games,Adding new food types at all,,2,The selection rule and the specific creators it picks between are welded together. Passing the creators into the constructor would separate 'every Nth one is special' from 'special means bonus'.,2

